{
  "summary": "Hardens legacy wallet migration to SQLite and descriptor wallets against mid-process failures and unexpected exceptions. The migration process now stages the new SQLite database in a temporary directory and only replaces the original BerkeleyDB file once records are fully written. Additionally, exceptions during migration and sub-wallet creation are caught to return graceful errors and allow automatic restoration of the original wallet.",
  "problem": "During legacy wallet migration, MigrateToSQLite previously deleted the original BerkeleyDB database before ensuring the new SQLite database was successfully populated. Furthermore, filesystem errors (such as read-only directories) or exceptions during wallet descriptor creation abruptly aborted execution and bypassed cleanup routines, leaving wallets in an inconsistent state that required manual user recovery from backup.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: 'The goal of the PR is to handle failures in a controlled way. Just so the process can automatically restore the original wallet without requiring user manual intervention.'",
    "Commit 20a4aa4ad2: 'If creation fails, the wallet ends up partly in sqlite format with legacy records, and the user has to restore from a backup manually. This fixes all that by creating the sqlite db in a temporary directory first. Then, the original BDB files are removed only once the sqlite db is fully constructed...'"
  ],
  "dependencies": {
   "depends_on": [],
   "enables": []
  },
  "scope_notes": "Refactors duplicate creation logic for watch-only and solvables wallets into a single helper and adds filesystem copy helpers to src/util/fs.h.",
  "changed_since_previous": "Now includes concrete implementation diffs for the temporary SQLite database staging, exception wrappers in migration routines, and functional tests for permission errors and cross-filesystem migration.",
  "card": "This PR hardens legacy wallet migration against mid-process crashes and filesystem failures. It prevents database corruption by staging new SQLite databases in temporary files before removing original BerkeleyDB databases, and catches exceptions during migration to ensure automatic cleanup and recovery. This prevents users from being left with corrupt or half-migrated wallets requiring manual backup restoration. It touches wallet storage logic and currently needs a rebase.",
